ut I only have one J1 left. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a concealed Pung or Kong. But I don't have three of any other tile. \n\nThis seems problematic. So Penging J1 might not lead to a winning hand unless I draw a specific tile. For example, if I draw a J1, making four, which would be a Kong. But that's unlikely. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can discard a tile and wait for a specific tile. But the problem is that after Penging, I have to discard a tile, which might not help. \n\nOn the other hand, if I don't Peng and keep the J1 pair, maybe I can form a different hand. Let's see. Original hand: [W1, W2, W9, W9, B1, B5, B9, F4, J1, J1]. Meld [W2, W3, W4]. \n\nIf I keep the J1 pair, then I need to form two more melds. The remaining tiles are W1, W9, W9, B1, B5, B9, F4. \n\nPossible melds: \n\n- W9, W9 as a pair. Then the other tiles: W1, B1, B5, B9, F4. These could form a pair and some melds? \n\nNot really. Maybe a pair of B9s? But I only have one B9. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a way to form a concealed meld. For example, if I have W1 and W9, but no middle tiles. \n\nThis also seems difficult. \n\nWait, maybe I can form a different structure. For example, if I have a pair of J1s, and then two melds. The remaining seven tiles need to form two melds and a pair. \n\nBut with the current tiles, it's not possible. \n\nSo maybe the best option is to Pass and not Peng, because Penging J1 doesn't help form a valid winning hand. Alternatively, maybe there's a way I'm missing. \n\nAnother angle: the scoring. If I Peng J1, I get a Dragon Pung (2 points). Also, since the seat wind is West, if I have a Pung of West Wind, but I don't. Prevailing wind is North. So no Prevalent Wind or Seat Wind points here. \n\nBut the hand might need more points. The minimum is 8 points. If I can't form other patterns, maybe this is not enough. \n\nAlternatively, if I don't Peng, maybe I can form other patterns. For example, if I have a pair of J1s, and maybe some other melds. \n\nBut